// proto-Blob — a lockless single-writer blob store with fenced atomic
// writes, built from today's primitives. This is the north-star example of
// docs/design/rfc-verification.md ("Acceptance test: Blob is a stdlib
// module"), written with what has actually shipped: entities, entity
// placement (`at`), compile-time invariant discharge, and typed errors.
//
// The protocol: the authority owns the blob and a fencing epoch. A writer
// asks for a WriteGrant; minting a grant advances the epoch, so every new
// grant silently invalidates all older ones. A write presents its grant,
// and the authority compares the grant's token against the current epoch
// AT THE POINT OF EFFECT — a stale token is rejected with a typed error
// before any byte lands. No locks, no client-side coordination: at most
// one writer's grant is current, so writes are single-writer by fencing.
//
// Each mechanism owns exactly one guarantee (the RFC's ownership table):
//
//   ATOMICITY  — the `object` construct. BlobAuthority is an entity: its
//                methods serialize (one message at a time, per instance),
//                so apply() can never interleave with grant_write() or
//                another apply(). No torn writes, by construct.
//   SAFETY     — the fence: `grant.token != self.epoch` checked inside
//                apply(), at the authority, at the point of effect. A
//                client-side "is my grant still valid?" check could never
//                give this (the world may move between check and act);
//                the authority's own compare is the only sound gate.
//                COMPILER-CHECKED: the `guarded_by` clause on `data` makes
//                "no write escapes the fence" a compile-time dominance
//                proof — remove the fence (or move the write above it, or
//                put a call between them) and this file stops compiling.
//   LIVENESS   — the grant itself. Holding the newest grant means your
//                writes get through; it promises progress, never safety.
//                (A lease deadline would also live here — a liveness
//                courtesy, never load-bearing.)
//   DISCIPLINE — typed errors. A fenced rejection raises StaleGrant, and
//                `at`'s mandatory-handling contract makes callers face it:
//                losing the race to a newer writer is a typed, inspectable
//                outcome, not a silent no-op or a crash.
//
// Epistemically (docs/design/epistemics.md): a grant is EVIDENCE — a value
// recording a past interaction with the authority ("at mint time, my token
// was current"), honest about its own staleness. That is why WriteGrant is
// a class (a belief, copyable, comparable) while BlobAuthority is an
// object (the referent itself — never held, only asked). And it is why the
// grant carries no `Valid` typestate: validity is a remote belief the
// world can falsify between any two instructions, exactly the thing a
// static type must never claim (rfc-objects.md, "Typestate and entities").
// Typestate tracks what *I* did; the fence checks what the *world* did.

// WriteGrant crosses the placement boundary as a wire-shaped class value.
import std.wire
// Named proof bundles (rfc-properties.md slice 2): the authority SATISFIES
// std.verify's monotonic and fenced properties instead of hand-writing the
// atoms — same obligations, same provers, plus the property names become
// the type's exported facts.
import std.verify

// The fenced rejection — a definite failure: the authority itself refused
// the write, so the writer KNOWS its effect did not apply and can react
// freely (re-request a grant, surrender, escalate). The payload says who
// out-granted you: your token vs the epoch that superseded it.
error StaleGrant {
    token: int
    epoch: int
}

// Evidence, not authority: a plain value carrying the fencing token. It is
// deliberately inert — no methods, no state machine, no deadline (a lease
// window would bolt on here as pure liveness). Possession of the newest
// grant is the write capability; possession of an old one is worthless,
// and only the authority can tell which is which.
class WriteGrant {
    token: int
}

// The authority — an entity (`object`), not a data structure. There is one
// referent; everyone shares it; every interaction is a message it answers
// one at a time. Its epoch is the fence: grants are minted FROM it and
// judged AGAINST it.
//
// The safety theorem is carried by two NAMED properties from std.verify
// (rfc-properties.md slice 2 — acceptance test 2), instantiated by pure
// substitution into exactly the atoms slice 1 shipped:
//
//   monotonic(self.epoch)  ==>  invariant self.epoch >= old(self.epoch)
//       Every write to epoch INCREASES it: proven at every mut-self
//       method boundary (relative to the method's entry) and at every
//       foreign write site (relative to the pre-write state). Fencing's
//       safety story rests on exactly this — a minted token can never
//       become current again. grant_write discharges it
//       (epoch + 1 >= epoch); a method that decremented the epoch would
//       not compile, and the error would blame BOTH sides: the property
//       body in std.verify and the offending write here.
//
//   fenced(self.data, self.epoch, WriteGrant)
//       ==>  data guarded_by (g: WriteGrant) g.token == self.epoch
//       No write to data escapes the fence: every write to `data` — the
//       whole-program closed write-set — must be DOMINATED by a
//       conditional the compiler proves implies `g.token == self.epoch`
//       for some in-scope WriteGrant. The fence in apply() is exactly
//       that conditional: its fall-through fact `tok == self.epoch`
//       (with `tok` bound from `grant.token`) discharges the obligation.
//       Writes from outside the authority's own methods are rejected
//       outright — the fence cannot be bypassed from without.
//
// Same obligations, same provers as the hand-written slice-1 atoms — but
// the NAMES survive as the type's exported facts, assumable downstream
// without reading the bodies.
object BlobAuthority satisfies verify.monotonic(self.epoch), verify.fenced(self.data, self.epoch, WriteGrant) {
    data: bytes
    epoch: int
    applied: int   // token of the last write the fence admitted

    // Hand-written compile-time proof obligations (rfc-verification.md
    // phase 2): the compiler proves these at every construction and write
    // site — there is no runtime check. The second one is the interesting
    // single-state fact: "no admitted write ever carried a token newer
    // than the newest grant." It is provable ONLY because the fence below
    // is an exact match (token == epoch on the fall-through path), and it
    // survives grant_write because applied <= epoch < epoch + 1 — linear
    // arithmetic over the entity's own int fields, the decidable
    // fragment's home turf. (These stay hand-written: they are
    // protocol-specific facts, not reusable bundles.)
    //
    // What today's fragment still cannot state: anything off the int
    // fragment — e.g. relating data to the admitted payload:
    // 'invariant self.data.len() >= 0' is rejected at declaration
    // ("'.len()' — collection and method facts are not provable").
    invariant self.epoch >= 0
    invariant self.applied <= self.epoch

    // Mint a grant: advance the epoch and hand back a token equal to it.
    // The increment IS the invalidation — every earlier grant's token is
    // now < epoch, so the fence will refuse it. Note what this does NOT
    // do: it does not contact the old holder, revoke anything, or wait.
    // Fencing is constructive knowledge — the old write *cannot* land, so
    // nobody needs to learn whether it was attempted.
    //
    // The `ensures` (properties RFC atom 1) pins the exact relation, proven
    // at every normal exit: minting advances the epoch by precisely one —
    // never zero (a mint that invalidated nothing), never more (token
    // gaps). The two-state invariant above says the epoch never goes back;
    // this says a mint always moves it forward. Callers assume the relation
    // at the call boundary without re-deriving it.
    fn grant_write(mut self) WriteGrant ensures self.epoch == old(self.epoch) + 1 {
        self.epoch = self.epoch + 1
        return WriteGrant { token: self.epoch }
    }

    // The guarded effect. The fence and the write sit in one serialized
    // method body, so "check, then write" is atomic — the classic
    // check-then-act gap is closed by the entity's serialization, not by
    // a lock the callers manage. This compare is the ONLY thing that
    // makes the store single-writer, and it is judged here, at the point
    // of effect, by the authority — never by the (possibly paused,
    // possibly partitioned) writer.
    // The fence is an exact match, not just "not older": only the
    // authority mints tokens, so token > epoch is impossible and the only
    // real mismatch is a stale grant — but writing the check as != is
    // both a tighter gate (a forged future token bounces too) and exactly
    // what lets the compiler discharge the applied <= epoch invariant
    // from the fall-through fact token == epoch.
    //
    // (The store keeps raw bytes; the boundary carries string, the closest
    // wire-transferable carrier today — `bytes` cannot cross a placement
    // boundary yet.)
    fn apply(mut self, grant: WriteGrant, d: string) {
        // (The token is read into a local so the guard fact `tok == epoch`
        // is a named term the prover can carry to the write below.)
        let tok = grant.token
        if tok != self.epoch {
            raise StaleGrant { token: tok, epoch: self.epoch }
        }
        self.applied = tok
        self.data = d.to_bytes()
    }

    fn read(self) string {
        return self.data.to_string()
    }

    fn epoch_now(self) int {
        return self.epoch
    }
}

// The demo drives two writers by hand to stage the race. `at blob { ... }`
// is entity placement: the call runs where the entity LIVES. Colocated
// (this program) that is a direct call; if `blob` were a handle fetched
// from another process, the same expression would dial the entity's home.
// Either way the boundary contract is identical — which is why every `at`
// must be handled (`!` here, surfaced to main below): a domain boundary
// can fail in SOME deployment plan even if not in this one.
fn run_demo(mut blob: BlobAuthority) {
    // Writer A asks the authority for the write capability.
    let grant_a = at blob { grant_write() }!
    print(f"A holds a grant (token {grant_a.token})")

    // A's grant is current, so the fence admits the write.
    at blob { apply(grant_a, "A: first draft") }!
    let v1 = at blob { read() }!
    print(f"A wrote -> blob is '{v1}'")

    // Writer B asks for a grant. The mint advances the epoch — this line
    // alone is what invalidates A. A is not consulted and needs no
    // network round trip to be fenced out.
    let grant_b = at blob { grant_write() }!
    print(f"B holds a grant (token {grant_b.token}) — A's token {grant_a.token} is now stale")

    // A, unaware, tries to write again. The authority's fence compares
    // A's token against the current epoch and rejects — a DEFINITE
    // failure: A knows its bytes did not land. The typed catch is the
    // discipline: the race outcome is data, not a mystery.
    at blob { apply(grant_a, "A: sneaky overwrite") } catch err: StaleGrant {
        print(f"fence rejected A: token {err.token} < epoch {err.epoch}")
    } catch err {
        print("boundary failure")
    }

    // The blob is untouched by the rejected write...
    let v2 = at blob { read() }!
    print(f"after the rejection, blob is still '{v2}'")

    // ...and B, holding the current grant, writes cleanly.
    at blob { apply(grant_b, "B: the good copy") }!
    let v3 = at blob { read() }!
    let e = at blob { epoch_now() }!
    print(f"B wrote -> blob is '{v3}' (epoch {e})")
}

fn main() {
    let mut blob = BlobAuthority { data: "genesis".to_bytes(), epoch: 0, applied: 0 }
    run_demo(blob) catch err {
        print("unexpected boundary failure")
    }
}
